   "text": "I read the drawing region by region and assembled the SMILES from that.\n\n**Left side**\n- A 6\u2011methoxypyridin\u20113\u2011yl group is attached to a benzene ring.\n- On the neighbouring ring carbons, that benzene carries a three\u2011carbon chain, CH2CH2CH2, that ends at an ether oxygen on the central benzene ring.\n- Its other neighbouring ring carbon carries a CH2\u2013O link to a pyridine, which closes the macrocycle.\n\n**Macrocycle core**\n- The pyridine is 2,6\u2011disubstituted: the O\u2013CH2 link sits on one side and the central benzene on the other.\n- On the central benzene, the ether oxygen is ortho to a CH2 substituent and meta to the pyridyl attachment.\n\n**Right side**\n- The central benzene's CH2 leads to C2 of a benzimidazole.\n- N1 carries a CH2\u2013oxetan\u20112\u2011yl group.\n- The carboxylic acid sits at C6, on the carbon meta to the ring fusion carbon bonded to N3.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- The only drawn stereo element is the hashed bond from the N\u2013CH2 group to the oxetane C2.\n- The CIP priorities at that carbon are O > CH2N > ring CH2 > H.\n- With the CH2N group pointing away, the implicit H points toward the viewer. The O \u2192 CH2N \u2192 ring CH2 sequence then looks clockwise, so the centre is S.\n- In the SMILES this is written as `[C@@H]`, which I checked against the neighbour order CH2N, H, O, C3.\n\nThe ring\u2011closure labels are kept distinct so that each ring closes on the correct atoms.\n\nSMILES: COc1ccc(cn1)-c1ccc2COc3cccc(n3)-c3ccc(Cc4nc5ccc(cc5n4C[C@@H]6CCO6)C(=O)O)c(c3)OCCCc2c1"
